xmodel/sol-probabilities-0821.md:15:**Main sensitivity.** The strongest credible downward evidence short of an explicit counterexample would be an independently reproduced, characteristic-zero-compatible all-depth residue-A germ together with a plausible algebraization and Keller-pair bridge. The strongest upward evidence would be a valid global counterexample-to-sheet landing plus exhaustive all-sector closure, or a different general plane-rigidity theorem. Additional finite-depth mod-p survival by itself would move me only a few points.
xmodel/sol-probabilities-0821.md:25:**Estimate: 90%.** I interpret “solution exists” in a horizon-free, proof-object sense: there is a finite, independently checkable resolution in ordinary characteristic-zero mathematics—a proof if JC2 is true or an explicit polynomial counterexample if it is false—whose indispensable dependency graph does **not** include exhaustive construction, termination, or a verdict for the particular residue-A, B-frozen, no-log, PIN42, `W1W2 != 0` cell tower at unbounded depth. The solution may reuse general lemmas discovered while studying that tower; its verification may not require the tower verdict. This is not the probability that the campaign finds such a solution soon. Residue A is a narrow modular chart, and no theorem in the repository makes it the universal endpoint of every counterexample or forces global approaches through it. The repository itself contains logically different candidates—global ramification/concentration, Euler-characteristic and nonproperness arguments, and DC2. Conditional on JC2 being true, I put about 86% on some finite non-tower proof existing; conditional on JC2 being false, I put about 99% on a directly verifiable counterexample not requiring the discovery tower. With the 70/30 truth split, that is approximately 90% unconditionally.
